import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="EPA NSPS and Vedolux Wood Boilers | Smokeless Heat Blog" description="Please take a look at our newer posting for an update on where we stand regarding the EPA testing.">
  <Header />

  <main class="pt-24">
    <section class="py-16 bg-charcoal text-cream">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6">
        <p class="text-ember text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] mb-4">News</p>
        <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl">EPA NSPS and Vedolux Wood Boilers</h1>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="py-16 bg-cream">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 space-y-6 text-charcoal/90 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Please take a look at our newer posting for an update on where we stand regarding the EPA testing. All of the boilers currently for sale are EPA-certified models.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>For U.S. customers:</strong> The new <a href="http://www.epa.gov/residential-wood-heaters/fact-sheet-summary-requirements-wood-fired-hydronic-heaters" class="text-ember hover:underline">EPA regulations</a> are now in effect, so there is limited availability of new wood boiler models. Some used and demonstration models are available. Until the new EPA tests are completed, new boilers still in stock are available for commercial installs. The NSPS only affects residential-use boilers. The EPA rule does not apply to sales in Canada. Boiler models that have passed the new test will be available for residential sales.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>UPDATE: July 6, 2016</strong> — The most popular and best-selling Vedolux 37 boiler model was the first one tested to the new standards. The Vedolux 37 tests have been completed for submittal, with emissions levels passing the 2020 limits. The Vedolux non-lambda boiler models have passed the new EPA emissions standards, so we are confident the lambda models will also pass the 2020 levels as they move through the testing process.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Varmebaronen Pellmax UB pellet boiler is on the <a href="http://www.nyserda.ny.gov/-/media/Files/EERP/Renewables/Biomass/Qualified-Technologies-SCP.pdf" class="text-ember hover:underline">NYSERDA</a> and <a href="https://smokelessheat.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/hhcertifiedwood.pdf" class="text-ember hover:underline">EPA lists</a> of approved hydronic boilers.
        </p>

        <p>
          Most of the pellet boilers on the EPA approved list were approved through NYSERDA’s renewable heat program using the EN-303-5 test. NYSERDA-approved boilers are automatically accepted by the EPA. We used this process for the Pellmax UB pellet boiler. Unfortunately, EN-303-5 tests could not be used to qualify wood boilers for NYSERDA’s or the EPA programs.
        </p>

        <p>
          We began the process many months ago with the hope that all approvals would be finished by 2016. We had indications from EPA correspondence in 2015 that after the laboratory we planned to use for testing received its accreditation from the EPA, the lab would be able to submit already-performed EN-303 tests for consideration. However, the EPA has very recently informed this lab that it has decided not to consider prior test results. The Vedolux wood boilers will need all-new tests performed by the approved lab. This will take some additional time, but we will continue working toward this goal.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Vedolux boilers we sell were designed for cord wood and thermal storage. The previous official test method for hydronic boilers was designed primarily for outdoor wood boilers and was not a workable option. A new test method for boilers using thermal storage and cord wood as fuel was announced with the publication of the EPA NSPS in May 2015. Because this test method is so new, labs have only very recently been able to perform it. A few U.S. labs were unwilling at first even to offer quotes for this new test. That left our European-imported boilers designed for thermal storage at a distinct disadvantage. There was no viable EPA-approved test option prior to this time, and consequently we were never on the EPA Phase 2 list. Considering that the rule was announced in May 2015, there was only about half a year to get everything in place by January 2016. Our boilers have lower emissions than the current 0.32 lb per million BTU limit when converting EN-303 lab results, but the comparison is somewhat apples-to-oranges because the tests use different filters. This emissions-rate limit is required for all new residential boilers being sold now. Varmebaronen and SmokeLess Heat are committed to the U.S. marketplace and plan to have their wood boiler product line EPA-approved as soon as possible.
        </p>

        <p>
          The new EPA rule now in place is effectively removing some of the very cleanest-burning products from availability for the residential market in the near term.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is how we understand the conditions we find ourselves in and how our business is working toward obtaining the required approvals for the wood boiler product line.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>UPDATE: March 20, 2016.</strong> The test lab SP has been approved by the EPA to conduct the new tests, and the results should be ready in time for Varmebaronen-approved boilers for this year’s heating season. Preliminary calculations show that the current Vedolux models should be able to pass the 2020 limits, pending final results this summer. These tests represent a significant financial commitment by Varmebaronen to continue building the U.S. and Canadian markets.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-8 font-medium">
          Dean Zook – owner
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
